WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.180
The year was 2002.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.610
America was just a few months out of 9/11.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:13.700
We were at war in Afghanistan, and at war
against an abstract concept called “terror.”

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:26.320
The news was full of anthrax attacks, shoe
bombers, and nuclear weapons.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.649
With the whole country obsessed with terrorism,
George Bush was one of the most popular presidents

00:00:31.649 --> 00:00:33.540
in history.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.270
But there was a problem.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.520
Even though huge resources had already been
expended, none of the terrorist leaders—people

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.920
like Osama bin Laden or Khalid Sheikh Mohammed—had
been found.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.010
And Americans were looking for blood.

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:50.140
But in March of 2002, after months of waiting,
that finally changed.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.730
The U.S. got its guy.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:53.850
No, not Osama.

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.860
And not Khalid Sheikh Mohammed.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.070
But he was still really, really important.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:03.700
You see, for months the CIA had been talking
about this one guy.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:10.159
His name was Zayn al-Abedin Muhammad Hussein
– more commonly known as “ABU ZUBAYDAH.”

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:15.289
According to the CIA, this “Abu Zubaydah”
was one of the most fearsome terrorists in

00:01:15.289 --> 00:01:17.520
the entire world.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.969
He was born in Palestine, but he had gone
to Afghanistan in the eighties to fight the

00:01:20.969 --> 00:01:21.969
Soviets.

00:01:21.969 --> 00:01:26.359
And after the war, he stayed in Afghanistan,
becoming the manager of a terrorist training

00:01:26.359 --> 00:01:31.429
camp, and the chief of operations for all
of al-Qaeda, and a top lieutenant to Osama

00:01:31.429 --> 00:01:32.710
bin Laden.

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:35.850
So Abu Zubaydah was a really big fish.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.340
CNN reported that Abu Zubaydah might have
actually been controlling al-Qaeda himself.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:45.109
Based on that information, the U.S. approached
the government of Pakistan, a country to Afghanistan’s

00:01:45.109 --> 00:01:51.409
east, and offered ten million dollars if they
helped find this mysterious bad guy.

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:55.520
So the Americans did raid after raid in Pakistan,
looking for Abu Zubaydah.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.319
After several attempts, they finally found
the guy in a safe house.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:04.209
During the raid, however, he was shot several
times in the head, suffering severe injuries—so

00:02:04.209 --> 00:02:08.569
severe the CIA flew a doctor from Johns Hopkins
into Pakistan to keep the suspect alive.

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:13.379
Once it was clear that he would live, the
CIA took custody of him.

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:16.280
In America, this was announced as huge news.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:23.349
“Among those we have captured is a man named
Abu Zubaydah, al Qaeda’s chief of operations.

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:28.040
From him, and from hundreds of others, we
are learning more about how the terrorists

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:29.890
plan and operate; information crucial in anticipating
and preventing future attacks.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.819
Our coalition is strong.”

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:36.590
But back in Pakistan, the people who were
detaining him discovered something disturbing.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:39.670
Abu Zubaydah was not exactly the guy they
thought he was.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:45.030
You see, while he was fighting in Afghanistan,
Zubaydah was hit in the head by some shrapnel.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:49.140
Some of that shrapnel lodged in his brain,
and gave him serious brain damage.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.280
He developed severe memory problems, and couldn’t
speak for a year.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.689
So the man they had arrested was not a “senior
al-Qaeda operative.”

00:02:56.689 --> 00:02:59.099
He wasn’t a senior operative in ANYTHING.

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:03.970
He was a greeter in a training camp in Afghanistan,
but it was not an al-Qaeda camp—it was opposed

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:04.970
to al-Qaeda.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:07.630
The CIA had even helped set it up in the eighties.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:11.900
When the government found Abu Zubaydah’s
diary—thousands of pages long, recording

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:17.800
minute details of people he met or what he
ate every day—they found nothing relating

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.000
to the terrorist attacks in which he was allegedly
involved.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.819
What they found instead was the story of a
mentally ill individual, whose head injury

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:30.700
resulted in his writing the diary in any one
of three different personalities.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:36.670
The FBI’s senior expert on al-Qaeda said
himself: “This guy is insane, certifiable,

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:37.670
split personality.”

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:41.519
The diaries showed that Abu Zubaydah simply
wasn’t involved in al-Qaeda.

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:46.640
He wrote more about his deep attachment to
his mother than about any involvement in terrorist

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:47.640
activities.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.349
Here’s one diary entry.

00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:54.629
“Now, I don’t cry when I remember my dad
beating up my mom.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.890
… I don’t cry when I remember her crying
like a child in my arms when I was a child;

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:06.290
I don’t cry when I remember all these things
as much as I cry when I remember that I offended

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.530
her.”

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.769
This was not exactly the “master terrorist”
the U.S. thought they had captured.

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:17.470
But the U.S. government had already said,
very publicly, that this guy was a top operative

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:28.440
in al-Qaeda and a trusted confidante of Osama
bin Laden.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.400
So in April 2002, President Bush turned to
his CIA director and told him, “I said he

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:34.400
was important.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.100
You’re not going to let me lose face on
this, are you?”

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:42.410
The CIA director responded that no, he wouldn’t
let the president lose face.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:48.850
So what was initially a MISTAKE, was declared
to be true—and became a FABRICATION.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:53.240
Abu Zubaydah became the first “high-value
detainee” of the War on Terror.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:57.350
Two weeks after his capture, he was transported
to a “black site” in Thailand, where he

00:04:57.350 --> 00:05:01.500
could be subject to a new method of interrogation
the CIA was testing out.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:06.350
Almost immediately after Abu Zubaydah was
captured, the CIA began discussing how to

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:12.230
interpret the U.S.’s prohibition on torture
in order to allow for what they called “enhanced

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:13.820
interrogation.”

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:17.290
And at the black site, they put that plan
into effect.

00:05:17.290 --> 00:05:21.080
Here’s how a government report described
the atmosphere of the prison.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:26.380
“A cable described Abu Zubaydah's cell as
white with no natural lighting or windows,

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:30.770
but with four halogen lights pointed into
the cell.

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:37.020
Security officers wore all black uniforms,
including boots, gloves, balaclavas, and goggles

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:41.701
to keep Abu Zubaydah from identifying the
officers, as well as to prevent Abu Zubaydah

00:05:41.701 --> 00:05:47.350
‘from seeing the security guards as individuals
who he may attempt to establish a relationship

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:48.950
or dialogue with.’

00:05:48.950 --> 00:05:53.530
The security officers communicated by hand
signals when they were with Abu Zubaydah and

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:57.160
used handcuffs and leg shackles to maintain
control.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:03.210
In addition, either loud rock music was played
or noise generators were used to enhance Abu

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.460
Zubaydah’s ‘sense of hopelessness.’

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:10.320
Abu Zubaydah was typically kept naked and
sleep deprived.”

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:14.380
During his time in Thailand, Zubaydah was
kept in terrible conditions.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:19.030
At the beginning, he was given no solid food,
and kept naked and shackled to a chair.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:24.800
After a few weeks, he was given rice once
a day, and allowed to sleep naked on the floor.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:28.980
Zubaydah was kept totally isolated for an
entire month while the CIA developed a plan

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:31.390
for more aggressive torture.

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:35.860
In late July, they began testing a procedure
called “WATERBOARDING,” in which a prisoner

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:39.990
would be strapped to a table with water poured
over their head, in order to simulate the

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.700
experience of drowning.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.740
In August 2002 alone, Abu Zubaydah was waterboarded
83 times.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.914
On one occasion, his interrogators continued
the torture for too long, nearly drowning

00:06:47.914 --> 00:06:48.914
Abu Zubaydah.

00:06:48.914 --> 00:06:53.420
For seventeen straight days, the CIA used
a variety of techniques: waterboarding, slamming

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:58.960
him against a concrete wall, confining him
in a small box, depriving him of sleep, blasting

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.070
white noise, and keeping him in a freezing
room.

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:07.630
As the torture continued, Abu Zubaydah became
so broken and compliant that when an interrogator

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:11.700
snapped his finger twice, Abu Zubaydah would
lie down flat to be waterboarded.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:16.960
In at least one session, he became completely
unresponsive, totally desensitized to the

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:18.230
torture.

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:23.410
All of this was filmed by the CIA, and ninety-two
tapes were produced of what was done in Thailand.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:27.900
But while they were torturing Zubaydah, the
CIA began to find that he wasn’t really

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.980
able to provide them much information.

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:32.670
The CIA still thought he was holding back.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:36.010
So they continued torturing him.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:40.110
In order to prevent further torture, Zubaydah
told them what they wanted to hear.

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:44.660
So Zubaydah told them that al-Qaeda wanted
to attack the Brooklyn Bridge and the Statue

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:50.010
of Liberty, and that they knew how to build
a nuclear weapon and smuggle it into America.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:54.500
He even told them that al-Qaeda had ties to
Saddam Hussein’s government in Iraq.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:59.300
All of these false statements were used by
the Bush administration to justify expanding

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:00.810
the War on Terror.

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:04.730
Abu Zubaydah’s statements were a big part
of the intelligence that America cited in

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:06.760
invading Iraq the next year.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:12.530
But what Zubaydah was telling his torturers
was never actually useful.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:17.540
According to the Washington Post, “not a
single significant plot was foiled as a result

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:19.980
of Abu Zubaydah’s tortured confessions.”

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:24.130
Soon the CIA began to have doubts about the
information Abu Zubaydah was providing, and

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:26.630
about their methods in getting that information.

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:31.310
Some of them even requested that if Zubaydah
died during interrogation, his body be cremated

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.669
so that the torture wouldn’t be discovered.

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:40.190
So in 2005, a CIA agent named Gina Haspel—the
person who oversaw the black site in Thailand—ordered

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:44.590
the destruction of ninety videotapes of Zubaydah’s
torture.

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:51.260
In September 2006, Zubaydah left CIA custody
and was transferred to Guantanamo Bay, America’s

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:53.580
main detention camp for “enemy combatants.”

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:59.110
By then, the CIA had already concluded that
Zubaydah was, in fact, not even a member of

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:00.110
al-Qaeda.

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:05.420
By the time that Zubaydah arrived in Guantanamo,
the torture had fully broken him.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.650
And that was more than a decade ago.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:12.140
To this day, Zubaydah is still in captivity
in Guantanamo, the country that tortured him

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:14.240
having simply forgotten about him.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.070
It never mattered that they knew he was innocent.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:19.900
Just look at what happened to the people who
tortured Zubaydah.

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:39.200
Gina Haspel, who oversaw Zubaydah’s torture,
became director of the CIA in 2018.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:53.150
President Bush retired to his ranch in Texas.

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:56.430
And Abu Zubaydah, meanwhile, is still in Guantanamo
Bay.

00:09:56.430 --> 00:09:59.120
He has never been charged with anything.

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:03.780
More than ten years after the government concluded
he wasn’t a terrorist, he’s still held

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:05.360
in strict detention.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:09.100
“My mother,” he said in one letter to
the outside world.

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:11.610
“I have not seen her in so long.”

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:15.740
I’m Ismael Loutfi, for the Gravel Institute.

